{"title":"2024 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 18","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><dl><dd><i>The following problem is from the <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">2024 AMC 10B #18</a> and <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_12B_Problems/Problem_14\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2024 AMC 12B Problems/Problem 14\">2024 AMC 12B #14</a>, so those problems redirect to this page.</i></dd></dl>\n<div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem_18\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 18</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Sidenote:_Proof_of_Euler&#39;s_Theorem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Sidenote: Proof of Euler's Theorem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_2_(No_Totient)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2 (No Totient)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_3_(Binomial_Theorem)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3 (Binomial Theorem)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_4\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_1_by_Pi_Academy_(Fast_and_Easy_⚡🚀)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 1 by Pi Academy (Fast and Easy ⚡🚀)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_2_(Fast!)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 2 (Fast!)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_3_by_TheBeautyofMath\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 3 by TheBeautyofMath</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-10\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">10</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_18\">Problem 18</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 18\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>How many different remainders can result when the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/9/e59e2c6e83eb78cca610a5fd4070ae01c8d4ae60.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$100$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" />th power of an integer is\ndivided by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/8/2/b829a6f0810a25e9ca9f85d690f2bafd0f2c1773.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$125$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" />?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/f/52f4ba28662fc7e35a330339cf847387f6337fe7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A) } 1 \\qquad\\textbf{(B) } 2 \\qquad\\textbf{(C) } 5 \\qquad\\textbf{(D) } 25 \\qquad\\textbf{(E) } 125$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"382\" height=\"18\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>First note that the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Euler%27s_totient_function\" title=\"Euler&#39;s totient function\">Euler's totient function</a> of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/8/2/b829a6f0810a25e9ca9f85d690f2bafd0f2c1773.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$125$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/9/e59e2c6e83eb78cca610a5fd4070ae01c8d4ae60.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$100$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" />. We can set up two cases, which depend on whether a number is relatively prime to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/1/4/214f2b60a5b2eeaaeefb9c8e24e4a26255190a60.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$125.$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"30\" height=\"13\" />\n</p><p>If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> is relatively prime to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/8/2/b829a6f0810a25e9ca9f85d690f2bafd0f2c1773.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$125$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" />, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/5/095750599aa1fdca75f6b3564e1970078b81a693.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n^{100} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{125}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"157\" height=\"21\" /> because of <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Euler%27s_Totient_Theorem\" title=\"Euler&#39;s Totient Theorem\">Euler's Totient Theorem</a>. \n</p><p>If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> is not relatively prime to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/8/2/b829a6f0810a25e9ca9f85d690f2bafd0f2c1773.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$125$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" />, it must have a factor of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Express <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/1/7/21720b5b2f3bbb26a4e2eac0f4fb7818037a0778.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5m$\" width=\"24\" height=\"12\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> is some integer. Then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/a/a7a36eece64e73d383534b3d053967dcb4c6b755.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n^{100} \\equiv (5m)^{100} \\equiv 5^{100}\\cdot m^{100} \\equiv 125 \\cdot 5^{97} \\cdot m^{100} \\equiv 0 \\pmod{125}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"483\" height=\"21\" />.\n</p><p>Therefore, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/7/2/872c8999e2545a588a11a46c26df08f3423dd405.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n^{100}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"33\" height=\"17\" /> can only be congruent to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/9/c6973a7a946dcdeda42b671c7f53c7c8ea925e35.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 \\pmod{125}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"101\" height=\"20\" />. Our answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/e/b5e8656291085c388b48a0fa8da85aed21c483bf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"20\" height=\"23\" />.\n</p><p>~lprado\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Sidenote:_Proof_of_Euler.27s_Theorem\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Sidenote:_Proof_of_Euler's_Theorem\">Sidenote: Proof of Euler's Theorem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Sidenote: Proof of Euler&#39;s Theorem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Euler's Theorem states that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/1/b612f4443e207caceaf95b88599d8dc0403551a9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a^{\\phi(n)}\\equiv 1\\pmod{n}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"146\" height=\"23\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/a/edae70ddedf89e790936ed4035c5ca1480212e0f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\phi(n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"34\" height=\"18\" /> is Euler's totient function, which counts the number of positive integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/b/4/fb40f477bf288da878d1114b65d0ea850e4815d8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1\\le x \\le n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"78\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/7/ce72b26a95d51c1fa9ad8232c0b0fde2d6593f11.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd(x,n)=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"106\" height=\"20\" />. Note that this only works for positive integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/1/5/f15b45ab77f281de250d44f9bcb2f3f3cd476859.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd(a,n)=1.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"110\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p>Consider the set of distinct positive integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/3/5/53584dac7e8ef9fa7a7214cda25cabea89864f7a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S=\\{x_1,x_2,\\ldots x_{\\phi(n)}\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"170\" height=\"21\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/a/f/2afbb476ec93ba7df042abda2beb1f17d9ab2e4d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1\\le x_i\\le n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"86\" height=\"17\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/e/e/1ee2dc7dd68199cf6804663683a3cf139e8ce813.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd(x_i,n)=1.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"117\" height=\"20\" /> Multiply all terms by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> to create <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/b/fab311a2d451ff31e71f6a2e8fb4ed6eb1289f96.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$aS=\\{ax_1,ax_2,\\ldots ax_{\\phi(n)}\\}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"214\" height=\"21\" /> First, we prove that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/3/7/43747a974556b7dbded0582108834993ce2e5aad.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd(ax_i,n)=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"121\" height=\"20\" /> for any <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/7/b/67bc6daa9d6b964201d6cef60cbeb1ac5fd26ead.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" /> using contradiction. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> be a prime such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/f/bbf6c9ca706997e99f88b1d82b33acd66b34d7e5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p \\mid ax_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"52\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/0/0/b0051d8cd1a15ff83125aa243ce88bd746b9ef4a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p \\mid n.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"42\" height=\"20\" /> This means that either <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/9/e/49effd714e2e2e48f30508247323a394e94a0212.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p \\mid a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"35\" height=\"18\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/8/34800889d4b6e0eb2c0b4bfcb17d66657d41cb9c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p \\mid x_i,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"47\" height=\"20\" /> which is not possible since both <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/0/540e20f40567c412d771a14c2be73a34f1a40442.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd(a,n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"72\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/e/e/1ee2dc7dd68199cf6804663683a3cf139e8ce813.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd(x_i,n)=1.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"117\" height=\"20\" />  Next we show that no two elements in set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/4/0/b406e4bae5060b9499a6241c1c501afccf3f3ead.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$aS$\" width=\"21\" height=\"12\" /> are congruent modulo <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/d/9/bd923e41635af40a1abe58f230732f2280b0723a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/7/b/67bc6daa9d6b964201d6cef60cbeb1ac5fd26ead.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/0/ac0e6fa105993ce976db0dc241d59ab8e68e6efc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_j$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" /> exist such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/b/bcb328a2c71fd16f367c4267d98ffa695c92bfa6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ax_i\\equiv ax_j \\pmod{n}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"158\" height=\"20\" /> Multiplying both sides by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/7/9/67930e76449cd36820e3562e52c9998bf083d37f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a^{-1}$\" width=\"25\" height=\"15\" />(which exists) gives <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/1/a31545a3b9720731895114f148d855fbc4c84fdc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_i\\equiv x_j\\pmod{n}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"138\" height=\"20\" /> This means that if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/d/ecd7a78130166d0343eef5ce3c2115b8102947dd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_i \\not\\equiv x_j \\pmod{n},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"138\" height=\"20\" /> then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/a/1/ea1d1d907e372df24860f8baf0f03b6a668a8882.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ax_i \\not\\equiv ax_j \\pmod{n}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"158\" height=\"20\" /> Now we multiply all terms in each set, and note that each residue appears exactly once in both sets, so we have: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/a/0/2a047509ed4c2f17b418e13d35651765c77be810.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[a^{\\phi(n)}\\cdot \\prod_{i=1}^{\\phi(n)} x_i \\equiv \\prod_{i=1}^{\\phi(n)} x_i \\pmod{n}.\\]\" width=\"252\" height=\"53\" /> Because each <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/7/b/67bc6daa9d6b964201d6cef60cbeb1ac5fd26ead.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" /> has the property that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/a/dcaab85b8567ce7c0f80e3b473df8f7d6eefcc29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd(x_i,n)=1,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"117\" height=\"20\" /> we must have that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/8/c/48c8b0d9d4d7cdd0ec14cc8ec1aa1608a72c3004.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd(\\prod_{i=1}^{\\phi(n)} x_i,n)=1.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"146\" height=\"58\" /> Then multiplying both sides by the inverse of product thereof gives <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/a/74af9af518d580403c279e1e248cf1c256f4583f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a^{\\phi(n)}\\equiv 1\\pmod{n}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"151\" height=\"23\" /> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/b/3bbb9e25e5be095e317771a9c5d00877ee9c8012.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\blacksquare$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>~nevergonnagiveup\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_2_.28No_Totient.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2_(No_Totient)\">Solution 2 (No Totient)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2 (No Totient)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Note that\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/7/5d7727afc558493f3ff5c0886691406de554f427.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(5+n)^{100} = {100 \\choose 0} 5^{100} + {100 \\choose 1} 5^{99} n + {100 \\choose 2} 5^{98} n^2 + \\cdots + {100 \\choose 97} 5^3 n^{97} + {100 \\choose 98} 5^2 n^{98} + {100 \\choose 99} 5 n^{99} + {100 \\choose 100} n^{100}.\\]\" width=\"857\" height=\"53\" />\n</p><p>Taking this mod <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/8/2/b829a6f0810a25e9ca9f85d690f2bafd0f2c1773.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$125$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" />, we can ignore most of the terms except the for the last <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/f/5df4b0edc2171c15c0b53ae849e3b0bda0526735.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[{100 \\choose 98} 5^2 n^{98} + {100 \\choose 99} 5 n^{99} + {100 \\choose 100} n^{100} \\equiv 4950 \\cdot 5^2 n^{98} + 100 \\cdot 5 n^{99} + n^{100} \\equiv n^{100} \\pmod {125},\\]\" width=\"691\" height=\"53\" />\n</p><p>so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/7/e872198365ba2d0e1a3da8fc2769cba23a9e8d53.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(5+n)^{100} \\equiv n^{100}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"135\" height=\"21\" />. Substituting <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/e/6ee5df0d5b6736df591c2c12ce45cf2d71bba5e7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-n$\" width=\"24\" height=\"8\" /> for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />, we get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/0/d20ae7cbd628f547af477d70f38682b194ca3a17.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(5-n)^{100} \\equiv n^{100}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"135\" height=\"21\" />. Therefore, the remainders when divided by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/8/2/b829a6f0810a25e9ca9f85d690f2bafd0f2c1773.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$125$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" /> repeat every <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> integers, so we only need to check the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/9/e59e2c6e83eb78cca610a5fd4070ae01c8d4ae60.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$100$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" />th powers of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/3/823db59ba5d3dbe69d8abcd62cb81b17cd3ef605.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0, 1, 2, 3, 4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"77\" height=\"16\" />. But we have that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/9/6/d966f71c4f6b5ac9ec4d48c772d78e92a598c7e0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(5-1)^{100} \\equiv 1^{100}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"131\" height=\"21\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/3/8f3ab082b41028a5a0d449add93fc8f6545e9672.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(5-2)^{100} \\equiv 2^{100}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"131\" height=\"21\" />, so we really only need to check <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/5/5/3552814dfdb20f6a4408498c7abd63b292d0ed44.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0, 1, 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"43\" height=\"16\" />. We know that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/b/c/dbce1918021fbaf32f63344dd40acb93cfb5260f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0, 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"28\" height=\"17\" /> produce different remainders, so the answer to the problem is either <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. But <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> is not an answer choice, so the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/9/909612b1ac69ce3f3770f46e5cb13846ba6454ae.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(B) } 2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"55\" height=\"28\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_3_.28Binomial_Theorem.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3_(Binomial_Theorem)\">Solution 3 (Binomial Theorem)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3 (Binomial Theorem)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>~Kathan\n</p>\n<figure class=\"mw-halign-center\" typeof=\"mw:File/Thumb\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:2024_AMC_12B_P18.jpeg\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/5/5e/2024_AMC_12B_P18.jpeg/600px-2024_AMC_12B_P18.jpeg\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"600\" height=\"849\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/5/5e/2024_AMC_12B_P18.jpeg/900px-2024_AMC_12B_P18.jpeg 1.5x, https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/5/5e/2024_AMC_12B_P18.jpeg 2x\" /></a><figcaption></figcaption></figure>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4\">Solution 4</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Firstly, I interpreted the problem algebraically. In other words, let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/6/4/f64c96395619031dbe5b637faeb4c1fe609bd56c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${x}$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" /> be an integer. Since we're raising it to the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/1/1a1394d78fdbfc22ce6fd93066987eaa3ed3fa44.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${100}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" />th power and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/1/1a1394d78fdbfc22ce6fd93066987eaa3ed3fa44.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${100}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" /> is even, we don't have to worry about negative values of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/6/4/f64c96395619031dbe5b637faeb4c1fe609bd56c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${x}$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" />. Now, we break <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/c/a3c712f1186d50d2fe254d3aa9d9eeaae7753d99.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${125}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" /> into three factors of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/3/753a491145bba9dab104278c8d00c34894da8f77.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${5}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. I first considered this when we're dividing by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/3/753a491145bba9dab104278c8d00c34894da8f77.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${5}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Okay so we can write the problem as below:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/3/2e31e3f15c76495eab7b730df0af4272690de981.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^{100}\\equiv r \\bmod{5}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"120\" height=\"17\" />. Now, in the world of mod(5), everything is much easier to figure out. Take <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/b/abb37660012229763085777b97b6a5aaa6414f88.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x\\equiv 0 \\bmod{5}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"100\" height=\"14\" />. Now, obviously <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/e/ede504addc8bc8b3773c04d14f98b55b540006f1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^{100}$\" width=\"29\" height=\"15\" /> will bring up a remainder of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/4/b54467db3352a5465f0fcb1ddba4305ae62ce0c6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${0}$\" width=\"8\" height=\"12\" /> if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/6/4/f64c96395619031dbe5b637faeb4c1fe609bd56c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${x}$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" /> itself is a multiple of 5. Now, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/0/9/f0994226df6cb84e8d870890e8727f3e339e0879.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x\\equiv 1 \\bmod{5}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"100\" height=\"14\" />. In this case, notice how <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/4/0/0406e8f07cda71f5d70b73e824d868a0d7b06979.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1^{100}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"32\" height=\"17\" /> is congruent to 1 in the mod(5) world. So in this case, we would have a remainder of 1. Considering <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/d/5/cd519d23ebd7f73a4636f44341de415485625f92.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x\\equiv 2 \\bmod{5}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"100\" height=\"14\" />, it's a little more tricky. Notice <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/a/6/4a62d9023875220ea7eb3a22159656b4986e1808.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{100}$\" width=\"28\" height=\"15\" /> is equal to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/6/016d200a24057d39a85efc32bab36b9e065e7d6a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1024^{10}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"52\" height=\"17\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/4/f/04fa6c85cbd03ee2d5e3c52958d2c958098f2dbe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${1024}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"38\" height=\"14\" /> is 1 less than a multiple of 5. So <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/4/f/04fa6c85cbd03ee2d5e3c52958d2c958098f2dbe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${1024}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"38\" height=\"14\" /> will be congruent to  <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/9/269ed86f70fcbadd2dc15a485db6ba0e569a127b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-1 \\bmod{5}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"79\" height=\"16\" /> and raising it to the power of 10 will bring up a remainder of 1 yet again. Similarly, is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/3/c73be114946d90c33183a6a0b4d93619f5f9afaa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x\\equiv 3 \\bmod{5}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"100\" height=\"14\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/7/3/973d89b553584fc2e28d065da5bef96b73c75658.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x\\equiv 4 \\bmod{5}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"100\" height=\"14\" />, you would notice both these cases will bring up a remainder of 1 again. So in the mod(5) world, we seem to only have remainders of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/4/b54467db3352a5465f0fcb1ddba4305ae62ce0c6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${0}$\" width=\"8\" height=\"12\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/2/3/b23f11ae8aa89101a04c249e8bde56915f357ca2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Interesting!\nTherefore, since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/c/a3c712f1186d50d2fe254d3aa9d9eeaae7753d99.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${125}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" /> is just <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/5/8452cffe7a56958921e8ab96e98f22b24de29610.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> factors of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/3/753a491145bba9dab104278c8d00c34894da8f77.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${5}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, we may say that in mod(125), we would have the same exact remainders namely <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/4/b54467db3352a5465f0fcb1ddba4305ae62ce0c6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${0}$\" width=\"8\" height=\"12\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/2/3/b23f11ae8aa89101a04c249e8bde56915f357ca2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Therefore, we would only have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/a/a/8aa3b1dd1af64004ff42f195167a4c024ae54a07.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${2}$\" width=\"8\" height=\"12\" /> possible remainders. This brings an answer of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/0/8/2082bb151bc28de0db6e1c74bf4e5fc0309ffbdc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(B)} \\; 2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"56\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p><p>~ilikemath247365\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_1_by_Pi_Academy_.28Fast_and_Easy_.E2.9A.A1.F0.9F.9A.80.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_1_by_Pi_Academy_(Fast_and_Easy_⚡🚀)\">Video Solution 1 by Pi Academy (Fast and Easy ⚡🚀)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 1 by Pi Academy (Fast and Easy ⚡🚀)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/c6nhclB5V1w?feature=shared\">https://youtu.be/c6nhclB5V1w?feature=shared</a>\n</p><p>~ Pi Academy\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_2_.28Fast.21.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_2_(Fast!)\">Video Solution 2 (Fast!)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 2 (Fast!)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=S7l_Yv2Sd7E\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=S7l_Yv2Sd7E</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_3_by_TheBeautyofMath\">Video Solution 3 by TheBeautyofMath</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_18&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 3 by TheBeautyofMath\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/KhD5-zDNoKM\">https://youtu.be/KhD5-zDNoKM</a>\n</p>\n<ul><li>Please do not use this unless you have a lot of time and enough brain power to calculate a lot of Totient.</li></ul>\n<p>~IceMatrix ~Marshall_Huang (minor edits)\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span cl